## SNS Fan-out → Multiple SQS Queues

```
Order Service
    │ publishes to
    ▼
[SNS Topic: order-events]
    ├──→ [SQS: inventory-queue]     → Inventory Service
    ├──→ [SQS: notification-queue]  → Email/Push Service
    └──→ [SQS: analytics-queue]     → Analytics Service
```

## Spring Boot: Send to SQS

```java
@Service
public class OrderEventPublisher {
    private final SqsTemplate sqsTemplate;

    public OrderEventPublisher(SqsTemplate sqsTemplate) {
        this.sqsTemplate = sqsTemplate;
    }

    public void publishOrderCreated(Order order) {
        OrderCreatedEvent event = new OrderCreatedEvent(order.getId(), order.getCustomerId(),
            order.getTotalAmount(), Instant.now());
        sqsTemplate.send("order-events-queue", event);
    }
}
```

## Spring Boot: Consume from SQS

```java
@Service
public class InventoryConsumer {

    @SqsListener(value = "inventory-queue", acknowledgementMode = MANUAL)
    public void processInventoryUpdate(
            OrderCreatedEvent event,
            Acknowledgement ack) {
        try {
            inventoryService.reserveItems(event.getOrderId());
            ack.acknowledge();  // delete message from queue
        } catch (InsufficientStockException e) {
            log.error("Insufficient stock for order {}", event.getOrderId());
            // DON'T acknowledge — message becomes visible after visibility timeout
            // After maxReceiveCount retries → goes to DLQ
        }
    }
}
```

## DLQ Configuration

```java
// After 3 processing failures, message goes to DLQ
// Configure via AWS Console or CloudFormation:
// redrive_policy: {deadLetterTargetArn: inventory-dlq-arn, maxReceiveCount: 3}

@SqsListener("inventory-dlq")
public void handleDeadLetter(OrderCreatedEvent event) {
    log.error("DEAD LETTER: order {} could not be processed", event.getOrderId());
    alertingService.notify("Dead letter received: " + event.getOrderId());
    // Store for manual investigation
    deadLetterRepo.save(new DeadLetterRecord(event));
}
```

The power of Spring Cloud Function is that your function bean is a standard Spring component. It has access to dependency injection, `@ConfigurationProperties`, Spring Data repositories — everything you'd use in a normal Spring Boot application. The Lambda-specific adapter is a deployment concern, not a code concern.

The function signature determines the Lambda input/output mapping. For an API Gateway HTTP trigger, the input is the request body; for SQS, it's the message payload.

```java
@SpringBootApplication
public class OrderProcessorApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(OrderProcessorApp.class, args);
    }

    @Bean
    public Function<OrderEvent, OrderResult> processOrder(OrderService orderService) {
        return event -> {
            var order = orderService.validate(event);
            orderService.save(order);
            return new OrderResult(order.id(), "PROCESSED");
        };
    }
}
```

The adapter dependency and the handler class name are the only Lambda-specific configuration. The `FunctionInvoker` class from the adapter bridges Lambda's handler interface to your Spring function bean.

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.cloud</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-cloud-function-adapter-aws</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

In the Lambda configuration, set the handler to `org.springframework.cloud.function.adapter.aws.FunctionInvoker::handleRequest`. Spring Cloud Function discovers your function bean by name — if you have exactly one `Function` bean, it's used automatically.

The most cost-effective Lambda pattern for Spring Boot isn't API Gateway HTTP triggers — it's **SQS batch processing**. Lambda polls the SQS queue, batches up to 10 messages per invocation, and processes them in a single function call. This amortizes the invocation cost and initialization overhead across multiple messages.

Spring Cloud Function handles SQS batch events with a `Function<List<SQSEvent.SQSMessage>, Void>` signature. The adapter deserializes the batch automatically.

```java
@Bean
public Function<List<OrderEvent>, Void> processBatch(OrderService orderService) {
    return events -> {
        events.forEach(event -> {
            try {
                orderService.process(event);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                log.error("Failed to process order {}", event.orderId(), e);
                throw e; // failed messages return to queue for retry
            }
        });
        return null;
    };
}
```

Configure the SQS event source mapping with `batchSize` and `maxBatchingWindowSeconds` to control throughput vs latency. A batch size of 10 with a 5-second window means Lambda waits up to 5 seconds to collect up to 10 messages before invoking — maximizing batch efficiency during high-traffic periods while keeping latency bounded during low-traffic.

The cost comparison with ECS Fargate is the decision point. Lambda charges per invocation ($0.20/million) plus per-GB-second of memory. For bursty workloads with idle periods, Lambda with SnapStart is dramatically cheaper because you pay nothing during idle. For sustained high-throughput (>1000 messages/second continuously), Fargate's flat per-second pricing becomes cheaper because you avoid per-invocation charges.

Some scenarios require server-side file processing — virus scanning before storage, image resizing, or watermarking. In these cases, stream the file instead of buffering it entirely in memory.

The AWS SDK v2 `S3Client` supports both approaches. For uploads, `RequestBody.fromInputStream()` streams directly from the multipart request. For downloads, `ResponseInputStream` lets you process data in chunks without loading the full object into heap.

```java
@Service
public class FileStorageService {
    private final S3Client s3;
    private static final String BUCKET = "my-app-uploads";

    public FileStorageService(S3Client s3) {
        this.s3 = s3;
    }

    public String upload(String key, InputStream content,
                         long contentLength, String contentType) {
        s3.putObject(
            PutObjectRequest.builder()
                .bucket(BUCKET).key(key)
                .contentType(contentType)
                .contentLength(contentLength) // required for streaming
                .build(),
            RequestBody.fromInputStream(content, contentLength));
        return "s3://" + BUCKET + "/" + key;
    }

    public void download(String key, OutputStream target) throws IOException {
        try (var response = s3.getObject(
                GetObjectRequest.builder().bucket(BUCKET).key(key).build())) {
            response.transferTo(target); // streams without full heap load
        }
    }
}
```

The `contentLength` parameter on upload is required for streaming — without it, the SDK buffers the entire stream into memory to calculate the size, defeating the purpose of streaming. If you don't know the content length upfront, use the S3 multipart upload API which uploads in chunks.

Presigned URLs are the highest-impact pattern for file-heavy applications. Your Spring Boot server generates a URL that's signed with your IAM credentials and valid for a limited time. The client uses this URL to communicate directly with S3 — your server is never in the data path.

The `S3Presigner` (SDK v2) generates both download (GET) and upload (PUT) URLs. The expiry time is a security decision: long enough for the transfer, short enough that a leaked URL doesn't provide indefinite access.

```java
@Service
public class PresignedUrlService {
    private final S3Presigner presigner;
    private static final String BUCKET = "my-app-uploads";

    public PresignedUrlService(S3Presigner presigner) {
        this.presigner = presigner;
    }

    public URL generateUploadUrl(String key, String contentType, int expiryMinutes) {
        var request = PutObjectRequest.builder()
            .bucket(BUCKET).key(key)
            .contentType(contentType)
            .build();
        return presigner.presignPutObject(b -> b
            .signatureDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(expiryMinutes))
            .putObjectRequest(request))
            .url();
    }

    public URL generateDownloadUrl(String key, int expiryMinutes) {
        var request = GetObjectRequest.builder()
            .bucket(BUCKET).key(key)
            .build();
        return presigner.presignGetObject(b -> b
            .signatureDuration(Duration.ofMinutes(expiryMinutes))
            .getObjectRequest(request))
            .url();
    }
}
```

The client uploads with a simple `PUT` request to the presigned URL — no AWS SDK needed on the client side. This works from any HTTP client, including browser `fetch()`. The `contentType` must match what was specified during URL generation, or S3 rejects the upload with a `SignatureDoesNotMatch` error.

For downloads, serve the presigned URL as a redirect (`302`) from your API endpoint. The client follows the redirect and downloads directly from S3's CDN-grade infrastructure — faster than proxying and zero load on your server.

The most common IAM confusion in containerized deployments is the distinction between the **execution role** and the **task role**. Both are IAM roles, both are referenced in the task definition, and both produce similar-looking permission errors — but they serve completely different purposes.

The **execution role** (`executionRoleArn`) is what **ECS itself** uses. It needs permissions to pull the container image from ECR and write container logs to CloudWatch Logs. This role has nothing to do with what your application code does.

The **task role** (`taskRoleArn`) is what **your Spring Boot code** assumes at runtime. When your code calls `s3Client.putObject()` or `sqsClient.sendMessage()`, the SDK authenticates using the task role's credentials. This role should have only the specific permissions your application needs.

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::order-uploads/*"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": ["sqs:SendMessage", "sqs:ReceiveMessage", "sqs:DeleteMessage"],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:sqs:us-east-1:123456789:order-processing-queue"
    },
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "secretsmanager:GetSecretValue",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:secretsmanager:us-east-1:123456789:secret:order-db-*"
    }
  ]
}
```

Notice the resource ARNs are specific — not `*`. The S3 permission is scoped to a single bucket and prefix. The SQS permission is scoped to a single queue. The Secrets Manager permission uses a prefix pattern to allow access to all secrets starting with `order-db-`. If this service is compromised, the attacker can access only these specific resources, not the entire AWS account.

IAM policy conditions add a second layer of restriction beyond actions and resources. They evaluate context about the request — the source IP, the VPC, the encryption status, or the tags on the resource — and deny the request if the condition isn't met.

The most practical condition for production: **VPC endpoint restriction**. This ensures your application can only access S3 from within your VPC, not from a developer's laptop using the same role.

```json
{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": ["s3:GetObject", "s3:PutObject"],
  "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::order-uploads/*",
  "Condition": {
    "StringEquals": {
      "aws:SourceVpce": "vpce-1234567890abcdef0"
    }
  }
}
```

Other useful conditions for Java applications: `aws:RequestedRegion` restricts calls to specific regions (prevents accidental cross-region operations), `s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption` requires encryption on uploaded objects, and `aws:PrincipalTag` restricts access based on tags attached to the calling role — useful for multi-tenant environments.

For auditing, AWS CloudTrail records every API call made by every IAM identity. IAM Access Analyzer can scan your policies and identify resources shared outside your account. And **IAM Access Advisor** shows which permissions each role has actually used in the last 90 days — the gap between granted and used permissions is your least-privilege improvement opportunity.

Adding the CloudWatch Micrometer registry exports all Actuator metrics — JVM heap, GC pauses, HTTP request latency, HikariCP connection pool, and cache stats — as CloudWatch custom metrics. No code changes to your application; the registry pushes metrics on a configurable interval.

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
</dependency>
<dependency>
  <groupId>io.micrometer</groupId>
  <artifactId>micrometer-registry-cloudwatch2</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

```yaml
management:
  metrics:
    export:
      cloudwatch:
        namespace: OrderService  # custom namespace, not the default AWS/ECS
        step: 60s               # push interval
        enabled: true
    tags:
      environment: production
      service: order-service
```

The `namespace` is critical — it organizes your metrics separately from AWS infrastructure metrics. The `tags` become CloudWatch dimensions, enabling filtering ("show me p99 latency for order-service in production"). The `step: 60s` balances cost and resolution — CloudWatch charges $0.30 per metric per month, and publishing 50 metrics across 5 tag combinations every 10 seconds creates 250 metrics × 6 values/minute = expensive.

For custom business metrics beyond what Actuator provides automatically, inject the `MeterRegistry` and record events directly.

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    private final Counter orderCounter;
    private final Timer orderProcessingTimer;

    public OrderService(MeterRegistry registry) {
        this.orderCounter = registry.counter("orders.created", "type", "standard");
        this.orderProcessingTimer = registry.timer("orders.processing.time");
    }

    public Order createOrder(OrderRequest req) {
        return orderProcessingTimer.record(() -> {
            var order = processOrder(req);
            orderCounter.increment();
            return order;
        });
    }
}
```

This gives you `orders.created` (counter) and `orders.processing.time` (timer with p50/p95/p99) as CloudWatch metrics, alongside the automatic JVM and HTTP metrics.
